flowchart TD
  %% author: Brad W. Setser | outlet: CFR Follow the Money | date: 2025-07-28
  %% title: China is also Fighting a Trade War with Europe (and Winning)
  %% url: https://www.cfr.org/articles/china-also-fighting-trade-war-europe-and-winning
  %% topics: China, EU, industrial policy, currency, autos | genre: blog / policy commentary

  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["`**Question**
      Why is Europe losing ground to China in trade, and what should it do about it?`"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["`**Stated assumption**
      China's trade war tools are industrial policy and a weak currency, not tariffs`"]
    P2["`**Stated assumption**
      Chinese firms, private or state-linked, accept low returns on equity and build too much`"]
    P3["`**Hidden assumption**
      *A trading partner's surplus drains demand from your economy; manufacturing capacity has strategic value beyond its profits*`"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    E1["`**Data**
      European exports to China down about 0.5 percentage points of European GDP; EU trade deficit with China set to double between 2020 and 2025; European auto exports to China on track for about $10 billion by 2027; Chinese electric vehicle exports at a 3 million annual pace in the second quarter; net exports added about 2 percentage points to Chinese growth over four quarters; yuan weaker against the euro`"]
    E2["`**Studies and sources**
      Centre for European Reform brief on German industry; Financial Times, New York Times and Wall Street Journal sector reporting; Adam Tooze; German customs data`"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["`**Overcapacity**
      Competing local governments add subsidized capacity, which drives profits down and pushes output China cannot absorb into exports`"]
    M2["`**Weak currency**
      State banks keep the yuan weak; its fall against the euro has roughly offset the EU's 17 percent countervailing duty on BYD`"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["`**Intermediate claim**
      The second China shock is eroding Europe's core export sectors and is large enough to matter for the whole economy, especially Germany's stagnation`"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["`**Main claim**
      China is winning a trade war with Europe; Europe needs a counter-industrial policy, broader tariffs on Chinese autos and new pressure for the yuan to rise`"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Counterpoints"]
    X["`**Opposing view**
      Europe's inadequate response so far; Trump-style tariffs or commodity purchase deals as the fix`"]
    QF["`**Caveat**
      The United States is still importing, which limits the damage, but American firms will not absorb tariff costs much longer`"]
    R1["`**Other explanation**
      China's long-standing tariff walls (25 percent on autos) also helped build the sector`"]
  end

  P1 --> M1
  P2 --> M1
  P1 --> M2
  E1 --> M1
  E1 --> M2
  E2 --> M1
  M1 --> C1
  M2 --> C1
  P3 -->|rule behind it| C1
  C1 --> K
  K -.->|pushes back on| X
  K -. caveat .- QF
  M1 -. other explanation .- R1

  Q ~~~ P1 & P2 & P3
  P1 ~~~ E1
  P3 ~~~ E2

